{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015qsMhiQFs8rx94cPJJm8mM","type":"tool_result","content":"  provider: 'auto', // or a concrete venue; defaults server-side\n  slippageBps: 50,\n});\n\nconst outcome = await client.swap(quotes[0]); // SubmitOutcome\n```\n\nMethod signatures:\n\n```ts\nclient.getSwapQuote(params: SwapQuoteParams): Promise<SwapQuote[]>;\nclient.swap(quote: SwapQuote, opts?: { autoTrustline?: boolean }): Promise<SubmitOutcome>;\n```\n\nThe standalone `quoteSwap(api, body): Promise<SwapQuoteContent>` export is available for advanced callers that already\nhold a `PollarApiClient`.\n\n---\n\n### Ramps\n\nA ramp is an order of one or more legs that Pollar runs server-side: the\nprovider's leg (fiat in or out), and a swap or a bridge when the asset or chain\nthe user wants is not the provider's. Pick a pair from the routes, quote it,\nturn the quote the user accepts into an order, and follow the order.\n\n```ts\nimport { describeRampAction, rampQuoteFields } from '@pollar/core';\n\nconst { routes } = await client.getRampRoutes('ONRAMP');\nconst { fiat, asset } = routes[0];\nconst { quotes, rejected } = await client.quoteRamp({\n  direction: 'ONRAMP',\n  fiat: { currency: fiat.currency, rail: fiat.rail, country: fiat.country },\n  asset: { code: asset.code, identifier: asset.identifier, chain: asset.chain },\n  amount: { value: '100', denomination: 'in' }, // 'out' fixes what the user receives\n});\n// Ask the user for rampQuoteFields(quotes[0]) and send the answers as `payee`.\nconst order = await client.createRampOrder({ quoteId: quotes[0].quoteId, payee: { name: 'Ana Perez' } });\nawait client.pollRampOrder(order.id, { onUpdate: (o) => render(describeRampAction(o)) });\n```\n\n- `pendingAction` says what the order waits for: `FIAT_PAYMENT` (a QR, already\n  rendered in `instructions.scannable`, or bank details), `SIGNATURE` (sign with\n  `signRampLeg(orderId, actionId)` from an explicit Authorize),\n  `COSIGN_APPROVAL` (vote on it like any co-sign request) or `KYC` (a link).\n- `nextPollAfter` is when to read the order again; `pollRampOrder` follows it\n  and stops once the order settles.\n- A failure carries `resolutionAction`; `rampResolutionText(failure)` turns it\n  into what the user can do. `rampLegTitle` and `rampLegStatus` describe each leg.\n- A payee the provider would refuse answers `400 VALIDATION_ERROR` with the\n  reason in `body.message`; a quote with no route answers `422\nSDK_RAMPS_NO_ROUTE` with `body.rejected`.\n- `listRampOrders({ pending: 'true' })` returns the orders waiting on the user.\n\n### Ramp adapters\n\nTo build a provider package for Pollar's backend, use `RampAdapter`, `defineRampAdapter` and `defineRampAdapterRegistration` from `@pollar/core` or `@pollar/core/ramps`. Install its server registration in the backend; its rails then appear in the routes above. Pollar retains credentials, persistence, lifecycle and reconciliation. See the [server registration guide](../../docs/development/custom-ramp-adapters.md), [provider implementation](../../examples/custom-ramp/adapter.ts) and [application example](../../examples/custom-ramp/client.ts).\n\n`RampChain` is a closed enum (`STELLAR`, `POLYGON`, `SOLANA`), independent of\nwallet networks. Use `RampChain.POLYGON` or `'POLYGON'` for a quote filter,\n----\n41:> resolve calls); typed KYC errors (`PollarApiError`), `KycStatus` gains `'expired'`, and\n43:> on manual review (`'pending'`) and on expiry (`'expired'`) - see [UPGRADE.md](../../UPGRADE.md).\n859:- `KycStatus` is `'none' | 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired'`. A read also carries `decisionStatus`\n860:  (`'pending' | 'manual_review' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired'`) and `reviewReason`, which say why a `pending` is\n862:- `pollKycDecision()` stops when the decision settles - approved, rejected, expired, held for manual review, or approved\n1023:// AUTH_ERROR_CODES.EMAIL_CODE_EXPIRED\n----\n647-fee) and signs only the sponsor; this client adds the new-account signature with the user's own wallet and broadcasts\n648-via the submit path. Not applicable to embedded (internal) wallets — created on the server at login — nor to smart\n649:(C-address) wallets. Trustlines are a separate step (`setTrustline`). The wallet exposes `existsOnStellar` +\n650-`fundingMode` so a UI can decide whether to offer this.\n651-\n652-#### `client.signAndSubmitTx(unsignedXdr?): Promise<SubmitOutcome>`\n653-\n654-Signs and submits in one call. This is the path smart-wallet (passkey) sessions use - it runs the passkey ceremony -\n655-and the argument is optional (it defaults to the transaction currently in `TransactionState`). Embedded and external\n656-sessions can call it too.\n657-\n658-#### `client.buildAndSignAndSubmitTx(operation, params, options?): Promise<SubmitOutcome>`\n659-\n660-Build + sign + submit in one call. External and passkey wallets keep the granular `building → built → signing →\n661-submitting → success` transitions (each composed call emits its own); embedded wallets take a single round-trip to\n--\n715-`EnabledAssetsState` (`getEnabledAssetsState()` / `onEnabledAssetsStateChange()`).\n716-\n717:#### `client.setTrustline(asset, opts?): Promise<TrustlineOutcome>`\n718-\n719-Establishes (omit `limit`) or removes (`limit: '0'`) a trustline for `{ code, issuer }`. Who pays is decided\n720-**server-side** from the app config: embedded wallets hit `POST /wallet/assets/trustline` (the server sponsors or\n721-self-pays, then submits), external wallets co-sign whichever XDR `/wallet/assets/trustline/build` returns. Pass\n722-`opts.skipSponsorship` to force a self-pay `change_trust`. Smart (passkey) wallets don't use classic trustlines.\n723-\n724----\n725-\n726-### Swaps\n727-\n728-Quote and execute asset swaps across multiple venues (SDEX, Soroswap, Aquarius). `getSwapQuote` returns one priced\n729-route per venue (each with a ready-to-run `build` payload); pass the chosen quote to `swap`, which establishes any","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0aa2e7a-ec07-4c51-b0b0-a33dc08a2abd","uuid":"527d4500-4a74-45e9-a93f-4595ed5ff88a","timestamp":"2026-11-09T22:30:58.718Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  provider: 'auto', // or a concrete venue; defaults server-side\n  slippageBps: 50,\n});\n\nconst outcome = await client.swap(quotes[0]); // SubmitOutcome\n```\n\nMethod signatures:\n\n```ts\nclient.getSwapQuote(params: SwapQuoteParams): Promise<SwapQuote[]>;\nclient.swap(quote: SwapQuote, opts?: { autoTrustline?: boolean }): Promise<SubmitOutcome>;\n```\n\nThe standalone `quoteSwap(api, body): Promise<SwapQuoteContent>` export is available for advanced callers that already\nhold a `PollarApiClient`.\n\n---\n\n### Ramps\n\nA ramp is an order of one or more legs that Pollar runs server-side: the\nprovider's leg (fiat in or out), and a swap or a bridge when the asset or chain\nthe user wants is not the provider's. Pick a pair from the routes, quote it,\nturn the quote the user accepts into an order, and follow the order.\n\n```ts\nimport { describeRampAction, rampQuoteFields } from '@pollar/core';\n\nconst { routes } = await client.getRampRoutes('ONRAMP');\nconst { fiat, asset } = routes[0];\nconst { quotes, rejected } = await client.quoteRamp({\n  direction: 'ONRAMP',\n  fiat: { currency: fiat.currency, rail: fiat.rail, country: fiat.country },\n  asset: { code: asset.code, identifier: asset.identifier, chain: asset.chain },\n  amount: { value: '100', denomination: 'in' }, // 'out' fixes what the user receives\n});\n// Ask the user for rampQuoteFields(quotes[0]) and send the answers as `payee`.\nconst order = await client.createRampOrder({ quoteId: quotes[0].quoteId, payee: { name: 'Ana Perez' } });\nawait client.pollRampOrder(order.id, { onUpdate: (o) => render(describeRampAction(o)) });\n```\n\n- `pendingAction` says what the order waits for: `FIAT_PAYMENT` (a QR, already\n  rendered in `instructions.scannable`, or bank details), `SIGNATURE` (sign with\n  `signRampLeg(orderId, actionId)` from an explicit Authorize),\n  `COSIGN_APPROVAL` (vote on it like any co-sign request) or `KYC` (a link).\n- `nextPollAfter` is when to read the order again; `pollRampOrder` follows it\n  and stops once the order settles.\n- A failure carries `resolutionAction`; `rampResolutionText(failure)` turns it\n  into what the user can do. `rampLegTitle` and `rampLegStatus` describe each leg.\n- A payee the provider would refuse answers `400 VALIDATION_ERROR` with the\n  reason in `body.message`; a quote with no route answers `422\nSDK_RAMPS_NO_ROUTE` with `body.rejected`.\n- `listRampOrders({ pending: 'true' })` returns the orders waiting on the user.\n\n### Ramp adapters\n\nTo build a provider package for Pollar's backend, use `RampAdapter`, `defineRampAdapter` and `defineRampAdapterRegistration` from `@pollar/core` or `@pollar/core/ramps`. Install its server registration in the backend; its rails then appear in the routes above. Pollar retains credentials, persistence, lifecycle and reconciliation. See the [server registration guide](../../docs/development/custom-ramp-adapters.md), [provider implementation](../../examples/custom-ramp/adapter.ts) and [application example](../../examples/custom-ramp/client.ts).\n\n`RampChain` is a closed enum (`STELLAR`, `POLYGON`, `SOLANA`), independent of\nwallet networks. Use `RampChain.POLYGON` or `'POLYGON'` for a quote filter,\n----\n41:> resolve calls); typed KYC errors (`PollarApiError`), `KycStatus` gains `'expired'`, and\n43:> on manual review (`'pending'`) and on expiry (`'expired'`) - see [UPGRADE.md](../../UPGRADE.md).\n859:- `KycStatus` is `'none' | 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired'`. A read also carries `decisionStatus`\n860:  (`'pending' | 'manual_review' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired'`) and `reviewReason`, which say why a `pending` is\n862:- `pollKycDecision()` stops when the decision settles - approved, rejected, expired, held for manual review, or approved\n10